Introduction

The Maryland State Retirement and Pension System (MSRPS) administers benefits for over 430,000 members, including active employees, retirees, and vested participants across 12 retirement systems—supporting the financial security of Maryland’s public servants.

Located in Baltimore’s Central Business District, MSRPS offers a purpose-driven workplace with modern amenities, including panoramic city and harbor views, renovated common areas, upgraded elevators, and an on-site café.

Employees enjoy comprehensive state benefits including; medical, dental, prescription coverage, generous paid leave, participation in the state pension system, and supplemental retirement savings options (401(k), 457(b), and more). Additional perks include an on-site fitness center with showers and lockers, 24/7 building security, and easy access to restaurants, shops, public transit, and major highways.

If you're seeking a career with impact, stability, and a supportive environment, explore opportunities with MSRPS.

This position is designated as telework‑eligible under the State of Maryland’s Telework Policy, after successfully completing the defined required training period.

GRADE

Grade 27

LOCATION OF POSITION

120 East Baltimore Street

Baltimore, Maryland 21202

Main Purpose of Job

The position will serve and support the mission of the agency by: Supporting the Chief Investment Office (CIO) on strategy, communications, governance and cross-functional coordination. The Chief of Strategic Planning and Administration (CSPA) will oversee Executive Operations, Culture & Communications, and the “One Portfolio” Culture. Executive Operations will include oversight of Executive support, staff coordination, event planning and management. Culture & Communications will include oversight of Investment Division (ID) culture, training and messaging alignment within the division and throughout the agency. The CSPA will be responsible for cultivating and nurturing the “One Portfolio” Culture along with the CIO and ID senior management. In addition, the CSPA will serve as a trusted strategic advisor to the CIO, exercising discretion, sound judgment, and professionalism. The CSPA will facilitate timely, transparent, and constructive communication between the CIO, senior management, staff, Trustees, and other stakeholders, and resolve or appropriately escalate issues while preserving access and reporting relationships. The CSPA will work to solve problems, mediate disputes, and proactively address issues before they reach the CIO. The CSPA will collaborate across agency divisions and units to advance strategic priorities and common agency-wide goals and objectives.

This position serves at the pleasure of the appointing authority.

POSITION DUTIES

The CSPA serves as the senior strategy and operations leader for the Investment Division and a trusted adviser to the CIO. The CSPA is responsible for strengthening the Division’s governance, Board and committee workflows, internal decision-making forums, administrative operations, personnel processes, communications, procurement, budget management, vendor oversight, travel administration, technology initiatives, and documented operating procedures.

Job Duties Include But Are Not Limited To

Chief Investment Officer (CIO) Support

Support the CIO on operational and investment strategy, communications, governance and cross-functional coordination with Investment Division (ID) staff and other stakeholders.
Oversee Investment Division Executive support, staff coordination, event planning and assist with the overall management of the Investment Division.
Oversee Culture & Communications including ID/agency-wide culture and communication alignment, and ID training.
Collaborate with the CIO and ID senior management to cultivate and nurture the “One Portfolio” Culture that includes innovation, long-term thinking and capability alignment.
Act as an intermediary/liaison between the ID staff and the CIO to solve problems, mediate disputes and proactively address issues before they reach the CIO.
Act as a liaison between the CIO and senior management, Trustees, and other stakeholders to ensure consistent communication, involvement and decision-making at the proper time;
Manage and coordinate organizational change and strategic initiatives across the Investment Division;
Plan, coordinate and manage internal and external organizational events;
Use AI tools to develop presentations, analytics and communications for Board and Committee meetings;
Manage, coordinate and prioritize the schedule and calendar of the CIO and establish an effective working relationship with the Trustees and Public Member Advisors;
Coordinate or attend meetings for the CIO and ensure accurate meeting minutes taken, recorded, and appropriate processes and assignments followed for them to be actioned;
Represents the CIO in meetings and phone calls with internal and external stakeholders;
Oversight over the CIO’s correspondence including answering questions and directing internal projects alongside division heads;
Attend events, conferences, meetings with CIO, as needed;
Oversight over the travel schedule for the CIO;
Identify areas for administrative improvement and make actionable recommendations;
Act as a strategic advisor to the CIO and ID senior management team;
Draft letters, emails, reports, and other correspondence on behalf of the CIO;
Participate with the CIO and ID senior management team in strategic planning, policy development, and decision-making;
Ensure timely and effective completion of projects and initiatives of the CIO;
Create and maintain systems and processes to streamline operations;
Establish standards and procedures for hiring and managing the office staff and personnel for the CIO;
Research and recommend new and revised policies and strategies;
Collaborate with the Agency’s Human Resource department and other agency senior leaders to address ID personnel issues and priorities.

Manage Administrative Support Staff

Manage a team of administrative support staff to ensure critical administrative functions are completed accurately and on time;
Coordinate and facilitate consistent communication between teams and across the entire division;
Ensure cross-training, redundancy and succession planning across the administrative functions of the division;
Establish performance objectives to measure and improve team performance;
Oversight over team that coordinates and manages the planning, preparation and distribution of committee agendas and meetings;
Ensure minutes are recorded of relevant Committee meetings and ensure action items are included in the subsequent Board agenda for approval;
Assess performance and provide feedback to the administration support team through employee performance evaluations;
Review policies and procedures relating to travel, invoices, payment and reimbursement and recommend process improvements;
Manage administrative workflows and assign tasks and functions to administration team members as appropriate;
Budget and allocate resources to the administration team as appropriate;
Oversight of division’s vendor relationships and support RFP and procurement processes as appropriate;
Oversight of the staff responsible for maintaining office equipment and ordering supplies;
Ensure high standards of quality for any material distributed by the Investment Division, reviewing relevant material to ensure accuracy and consistency;
Assist investment staff with understanding State and agency travel regulations and policies;
Oversight of staff that prepares and processes SRA Requisition/Working Fund form for ordering supplies and equipment, subscriptions and renewals, membership and renewals, hotel accommodations, registration fees, etc. for the Chief Investment Officer, Deputy Chief Investment Officer and Investment Division staff. Pay for requisitioned item and/or invoice with corporate purchasing card (CPC).
Keep CIO informed of Investment Division’s spending on all budgeted items;
Oversight of monthly reconciliation of bank statement and procurement forms for credit cards issued in the Investment Division.

Special Projects

Following the direction of the Chief Investment Officer, responsible for assisting with the oversight of special projects as necessary and other duties as assigned.

Minimum Qualifications

Education
: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university.

Experience:
Seven years of progressively responsible experience in strategic planning, operations, program or project management, governance, executive administration, organizational development, public administration, financial services administration, or a closely related field.

Desired Or Preferred Qualifications

Applicants are not expected to have experience in every preferred area. We recognize that qualified candidates may develop relevant capabilities through a variety of professional, educational, public-service, military, nonprofit, technical, and lived experiences. We encourage applications from individuals who do not meet every preferred qualification, particularly those from historically underrepresented backgrounds or whose careers have not followed a traditional investment-management or management-consulting path.

The Investment Division values different perspectives, professional backgrounds, educational paths, communication styles, and approaches to problem-solving. We are committed to an environment in which employees are respected, able to contribute openly, and provided equitable opportunities for development, recognition, and advancement.

Preferred Qualifications

Advanced degree or professional training and experience in business administration, public administration, organizational leadership, project or program management, strategy management consulting, human resources, finance, procurement, law, communications, technology management, or a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of directly relevant education and/or professional experience.
Experience leading complex cross-functional projects or operational initiatives involving multiple stakeholders, deadlines, and organizational priorities.
Experience supervising employees, leading teams, or directing substantial workstreams.
Experience developing or improving policies, procedures, workflows, controls, or governance processes.
Demonstrated ability to prepare clear executive-level written materials, presentations, recommendations, and decision-support information.
Demonstrated ability to manage competing priorities, exercise discretion, and communicate effectively across organizational levels and professional disciplines.
Experience leading inclusively, establishing high standards of professional excellence, inviting/incorporating diverse perspectives, and promoting transparent and equitable access to assignments, information, professional development, recognition, and advancement opportunities.

Additional consideration may be given to candidates with experience in one or more of the following:

Board, committee, or public-sector governance
Investment management, pension administration, financial services, or another regulated environment
Procurement, contracting, or vendor management
Operating-budget development, financial administration, or budget-to-actual reporting
Human-resources operations, performance-management processes, learning and development, or organizational culture/transformation
Strategic and executive communications, executive briefing, speechwriting, or presentation development
Technology program management, digital transformation, or responsible AI use and integration
Travel, reimbursement, purchasing-card, or accounts-payable process improvement
Strategic and event planning
Policy manuals, operating procedures, records management, or internal controls
Change management, organizational design, or administrative-team restructuring
Professional credentials in project or pro

Department Information

&

If your goal is to build a career that makes a difference and builds greater public confidence in good government, consider joining the dedicated people of the State of Colorado. Our professionals strive to support state government and the citizens of Colorado. Customer service is a key component for every position in state government as is the focus on ideas and ways to redesign the delivery of services in making state government more effective and efficient.

In addition to a great agency and rewarding, meaningful work, we offer:

Distinctive career advancement opportunities throughout the state system;
Strong, secure, yet flexible retirement benefits including PERA Defined Benefit Plan or PERA Defined;
Contribution Plan plus 401K and 457 plans. Member Contribution Rates . Correctional Officers I-IV qualify as "Safety Officers" and are eligible for increased PERA benefits;
Medical and dental health plans;
Short and long term disability coverage;
Paid life insurance;
11 paid holidays per year plus vacation and sick leave;
Wellness program, tuition reimbursement, training opportunities and more;
Visit State of Colorado Employee Benefits. for more information.
This position is compensated on a bi-weekly pay cycle.

Who May Apply

A residency waiver has been granted for this announcement. Applications will be considered from residents and non-residents of Colorado.

Job Location:

San Carlos Correctional Facility

The resulting eligibility list may be used to fill future vacancies at this location

Description Of Job

Responsibilities include, but are not limited to:

Provides professional mental health services to inmates to include formal and informal mental health evaluations, diagnostic assessments, and clinical interventions;
Assures accurate Information System mental health needs levels, including Psychiatric and Developmental Disability needs level codes;
Provides group, brief, and individual therapy, and crisis intervention to include participation in 24 hour Mental Health On-Call Rotation;
Assures both emergency and non-emergency transfer for inmates experiencing acute mental health needs;
Provides clinical direction to non-clinical staff in mental health care and treatment of inmates;
Assures continuity of care for inmates by making appropriate referral and providing necessary information and documentation for receiving units, facilities, or agencies;
Documents both handwritten and electronically in an accurate and timely manner to provide comprehensive inmate mental health records;
Monitors inmate treatment and listens to inmate concerns and provides counseling and direction;
Promotes and is accountable for the safety and security of the clinical and correctional environment through conscientious observations, and sensitive item contraband control;
Informs supervisor of any violations of safety and security through the report writing process, attends training programs as assigned, including self-defense course to ensure self protection skills.

Residency Requirement

Minimum Qualifications, Substitutions, Conditions of Employment & Appeal Rights

This posting is open to current and non-current residents of the State of Colorado at the time of submitting your application. However, if you are selected and accept the position, you will be required to establish residence in the State of Colorado.

Class Code & Classification Description

C4L2XX Social Work/Counselor II

Education/Licensure/Certification/Experience

Minimum Qualifications:

Current, Valid Colorado License Or Registered Candidate As a

Licensed Social Worker (LSW) or
Licensed Clinical Social Worker (LCSW) or
Licensed Professional Counselor Candidate (LPCC) or
Licensed Marriage & Family Therapist Candidate (LMFTC) or
Addiction Counselor Candidate (ADDC)

Current, valid full license from Colorado in any of the above fields also meets the requirements for this classification.

Required Competencies

Ability to provide professional mental health or sex inmate treatment to inmates;
Individual must be efficient;
Able to participate in 24 hour Mental Health On-Call Rotation;
Excellent written and verbal communication skills.
Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to collaborate effectively across facility programs and with external agencies;
Ability to work independently;
Ability to provide comprehensive clinical documentation;
Must maintain required registration/permit/licensure as applicable to this assignment;
Ability to work with a diverse population of inmates;
Ability to provide professional mental health treatment as part of a multi-disciplinary team;
Ability to read and comprehend the English language;
Ability to effectively communicate fluently in English both verbally and in writing;
Ability to create accessible documents and technical content that follows plain language guidelines with accessibility in mind.
Self-control, a strong sense of ability to work in stressful, potentially violent situations;
Ability to remain alert, emotionally stable, and mentally resilient under pressure and observant throughout their shift;
Tolerance to stress, confinement, and high-stakes interaction.

Preferred Qualifications

Graduation with a master's or doctoral degree in a mental health field such as counseling, social work, marriage and family therapy or psychology from a program accredited by Council for Accreditation of Counseling & Related Educational Programs (CACREP), Council of Social Work Education (CSWE), American Association for Marriage and Family Therapy (AAMFT or COAMFTE) or American Psychological Association (APA).
License eligible as a Licensed Professional Counselor, Licensed Clinical Social Work, Licensed Marriage and Family Therapist or Licensed Psychologist.
Experience providing mental health treatment in a correctional environment.

IMPORTANT INFORMATION

The Assessment Process
: Your application will be reviewed to determine if you meet the minimum Qualifications (MQ's). Applicants that meet MQ’s will be included in the Comparative Analysis process. Colorado Revised Statutes require that state employees are hired and promoted through a comparative analysis which may include, but is not limited to, a suitability screening, Online Assessment, oral assessment, written performance assessment or it may include a review of your application material and your responses to any supplemental questions by a Subject Matter Expert (SME) to assess and rank applicants in order to establish an eligible list for referral to the hiring manager for final consideration.

Be sure the “Work Experience” section of your application is complete and specifically addresses your qualifications, experience, and accomplishments as they relate to the Description of the Job
, Minimum Qualifications
, Required Competencies
and Preferred Qualifications
as listed above. Resumes and “see resume” statements will not be accepted in lieu of the “Work Experience” section of the application. Failure to include adequate information or follow instructions by the closing date may result in your application not being accepted and/or may affect your score, rank or inclusion in the final pool of eligible applicants.

Employees who are rehired or reinstated within six months of their separation will return at either their previous pay rate if hired into the same class level or based on their time in the class series, whichever is higher. Those rehired or reinstated after six months' separation will be calculated using the same formula for new hires and promotions.

Please note that while this change in role may constitute a change in title and/or responsibility, salary adjustments are not guaranteed and may be influenced by factors such as internal pay equity, budget constraints, market benchmarks, or the individual’s current compensation level. Any changes to compensation will be determined separately and communicated accordingly.

If you have transcripts, a license or other relevant documentation regarding your qualifications, upload and attach copies to your application.

If an unofficial copy of transcripts is being submitted at the time of application, transcripts from colleges or universities outside the United States must be assessed for U.S. equivalency by a NACES educational credential evaluation service. This documentation is the responsibility of the applicant and must be included as part of your application materials. Failure to provide a transcript or credential evaluation report may prevent your application from moving forward in the selection process.

Under the Job Application Fairness Act (SB 23-058), applicants may redact information from certifications and transcripts that identify their age, date of birth or dates of attendance from an educational institution.

In order to process a background investigation, your application must include at least (10) ten years of the most current work experience unless experience related to the job for which you are applying is later than (10) years. Failure to provide this information could result in delays and/or elimination from the background investigation process.

Toll Free Applicant Technical Support:
If you experience technical difficulty with the NEOGOV system (e.g. uploading or attaching documents to your online application) call NEOGOV technical support at 855-524-5627 Mon-Fri between 6 am and 6 pm (Pacific Time). Helpful hints: if you are having difficulty uploading or attaching documents to your application first, ensure your documents are PDF or Microsoft Word files and second, close the document before you attempt to upload (attach) it.

**You are encouraged to print a copy of this announcement for your records**

Conditions of Employment:
All job offers are contingent upon a successful medical screening by a healthcare professional, certifying the candidate's ability to perform the essential functions of the position. Applicant must consent to hold harmless CDOC and its agents and staff from any liability arising in whole or in part from the testing and the use of this information in connection with CDOC’s consideration of application for employment.

Must be 21 years of age
Possess a valid driver’s license. Drivers must maintain a valid, non-restricted Colorado driver’s license, obey traffic laws, and be able to safely operate a motor vehicle. State drivers are expected to maintain a safe driving record consistent with Colorado Division of Human Resources Universal Policy Driving Standards . Eligibility to drive a State-owned vehicle, includes holding a valid Colorado driver’s license within 30 days of employment or residency change and meeting specific driving record criteria. Serious traffic violations, including DUIs or reckless driving, can disqualify drivers for up to seven years. A ll State Vehicles are monitored by telematics for safe operation and employees have no reasonable expectation of privacy while using an agency State Fleet vehicle.

Possess a high school diploma or GED
Former State employees who were disciplinarily terminated or resigned in lieu of termination are required to:
Disclose the details of the prior termination or resignation on the application.
Explain why the circumstances of the prior separation should not disqualify them from the current position.
Provide your employee number from your prior State employment. Absent extraordinary circumstances, prior disciplinary termination or resignation in lieu of termination and failure to provide this information will disqualify the applicant from future State employment with CDOC.

Have NO felony convictions, to include any actions defined in 5 U.S.C. 301; U.S.C. 509, 510; 42 U.S.C. 15601-15609. The CDOC shall not hire nor promote anyone who may have contact with an offender who has engaged in sexual abuse which includes acts, activity, and sexual behavior, in a prison or other institution as defined in 42 U.S.C. 1997; or has been convicted of engaging or attempt

